## Symptom

A session reads the tree and concludes that files or work "do not exist",
while `git status` says the branch is up to date with origin.

## Story

This entry came with Precedent at install; it was paid for upstream. `git
status` compares against whatever the remote-tracking ref held at the last
fetch, so a container that came up on an old clone reports clean and
current while being dozens of commits behind. It bit the upstream project
twice. Once, a local branch shared no commits at all with origin, and a
whole phase of merged work was invisible. Once, a five-day-old shallow
clone led a session to conclude that files landed a week earlier did not
exist. Nothing errored either time.

## Fix

Before concluding anything is missing or unfinished, fetch first:
`git fetch origin <branch>`, then `git rev-list --count HEAD..origin/<branch>`.
`tools/bootstrap.sh` warns about this at session start, including when the
fetch itself fails, so silence there means "checked and current", never
"not checked".
